<section class="about" id="about">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="section-label">About</span>

    <div class="about-grid">
      <div class="about-content">
        <p>
          Modern artificial intelligence has outgrown the hardware built to serve it. Every
          transformer inference and every edge-deployed vision model still moves its weights across
          the same von&nbsp;Neumann boundary that separates memory from computation — and under real
          energy and latency budgets, that boundary is where most of the cost disappears. My
          research is an attempt to work on both sides of it: the <strong>device physics</strong> of
          memristive memory, and the <strong>compute-in-memory architectures</strong> that turn those
          devices into usable accelerators.
        </p>
        <p>
          At <strong>IIT&nbsp;Madras</strong> I reformulated the voltage-dependent capacitance of a
          hafnium-oxide resistive-memory device as a chemical-kinetics problem and built a
          SPICE-compatible compact model for it. At <strong>IIT&nbsp;Roorkee</strong> I mapped a
          Vision Transformer onto a memristor-crossbar accelerator and treated its device
          non-idealities as constraints to design against, not noise to correct after the fact. And
          at <strong>CSIR&ndash;CSIO</strong> I learned what edge deployment costs in practice — laying
          out the analog front-end, the ADC interface, and the real-time software myself.
        </p>
        <p>
          I want to keep working at the interface where the interesting problems in this field
          actually live: co-designing the device and the architecture together, toward
          energy-efficient hardware for edge AI and dense, non-volatile storage on the same physical
          substrate.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-education">
